---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { legal } from '../config/legal';

const mail = `mailto:${legal.privacyEmail}`;
---

<Base title="Privacy — Amble" description="What amble's website and app collect, why, and how to take it or delete it." path="privacy">
  <Prose kicker="Privacy" title="What’s yours is yours." lede="This covers the amble website, its waitlist, the app’s beta and your Rep on WhatsApp. We’ll tell you when it changes.">
    <p class="plain">
      In short: on the website we keep your email to invite you to the beta. In the app we keep what you share so your Rep
      can listen for you. No trackers, no selling, no advertising. Take it with you or delete it whenever you like.
    </p>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      {legal.controller}, operated by {legal.entity ? `${legal.entity.name} (${legal.entity.taxId}), ${legal.entity.address}` : legal.operator}.
      For anything about your data: <a href={mail}>{legal.privacyEmail}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>On the website: what we collect</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Your email address</strong>, when you join the waitlist, and whether you joined as a candidate or a company.</li>
      <li><strong>The language</strong> of the page you signed up from, to write to you in it.</li>
      <li><strong>When</strong> you signed up, as proof of your consent.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Your IP address is used for a few minutes, in memory, to stop abuse of the form. It is never stored. We don’t ask
      for, and don’t accept, anything else: the form rejects any field other than your email.
    </p>

    <h2>On the website: why, and on what basis</h2>
    <p>
      To invite you to the closed beta of Amble and tell you about it. Nothing else. The legal basis is your consent
      (GDPR art. 6.1.a), given when you submit the form. You can withdraw it at any time.
    </p>

    <h2>On the website: who else handles it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Railway</strong> hosts the website and the database where your email is stored.</li>
      <li><strong>Resend</strong> sends the welcome email and, later, your beta invitation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Both act on our instructions only and may process data outside the European Economic Area, under the European
      Commission’s standard contractual clauses or the EU–US Data Privacy Framework.
    </p>

    <h2>On the website: how long we keep it</h2>
    <p>
      We keep it until the beta ends or until you ask us to delete it, whichever comes first.
    </p>

    <h2>In the app: what we keep</h2>
    <p>If you have an account in the app’s beta (app.amble.fyi), we keep what your Rep needs to know you and listen for you:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Your account</strong>: your email, what you want to be called and your language.</li>
      <li><strong>What you share</strong>: your CV, your LinkedIn profile and any other document or text you upload, and the text we read from them.</li>
      <li><strong>What you answer</strong> in the conversation with your Rep, and what it learns from it: your capabilities, the evidence behind them, and what you look for or rule out.</li>
      <li><strong>The offers</strong> your Rep reads for you, why it shows or sets each one aside, and what you decide about them.</li>
      <li><strong>Your applications</strong>: the answers it prepares, the ones you write and the CV you attach.</li>
      <li><strong>What you tell us</strong> with the feedback button.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>In the app: why, and on what basis</h2>
    <p>
      To give you the service you ask for: understand your professional profile, read offers for you, show you only the ones
      worth your attention and help you apply. The legal basis is providing that service (GDPR art. 6.1.b), which you
      accept when you create your account. We don’t sell your data, don’t use it for advertising and don’t show it to any
      company unless you decide to.
    </p>

    <h2>In the app: a model reads for you</h2>
    <p>
      A language model reads your documents and every offer, and decides whether to show it to you. It always tells you why,
      and you can also see what it set aside and the reason. That decision only filters what we show you: it never sends an
      application for you or decides anything about you in front of anyone. If you disagree with how it read you, tell us and
      we’ll look at it.
    </p>

    <h2>In the app: who else handles it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Railway</strong> hosts the app and its database in the European Union (Amsterdam).</li>
      <li><strong>Helmcode</strong> runs the language model, on infrastructure in the European Union. It keeps nothing we send it and trains no models on it.</li>
      <li><strong>Resend</strong> sends the emails with your sign-in code.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>In the app: how long, and how to take it or delete it</h2>
    <p>
      For as long as you have an account. In the app, under “Your data”, you can download everything we keep about you as a
      file and delete your account whenever you like: it goes at once, with your documents. Daily backups are kept for 7
      days, so after 7 days nothing is left.
    </p>

    <h2>On WhatsApp: only if you link it</h2>
    <p>
      Your Rep can message you on WhatsApp, but only after you link your number: from the app you send us a WhatsApp
      message with a code we give you, and from then on we write to that number. Without that step, we never message you on
      WhatsApp.
    </p>

    <h2>On WhatsApp: what we handle, and why</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Your phone number</strong> and the identifier WhatsApp gives us for you, so we know it’s you.</li>
      <li><strong>The messages</strong>, both ways: what you write, the buttons you tap, the offers we send you and what your Rep answers, along with the reminders it leaves itself to write to you later.</li>
      <li><strong>Your voice notes</strong>, which we turn into text so your Rep can understand them. We keep the text as your message; the audio isn’t kept.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      It’s for the same things as the app: sending you the offers worth your attention, asking how your applications are
      going and helping with your search when you write. The legal basis is the same, providing the service (GDPR art.
      6.1.b), and linking WhatsApp is optional. If something you write is a problem or a suggestion, your Rep passes it to
      the team, and the people who run amble read it.
    </p>

    <h2>On WhatsApp: who else handles it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Zernio</strong> (<a href="https://zernio.com">zernio.com</a>) sends and receives the messages for us.</li>
      <li><strong>Meta</strong>, through the WhatsApp Business Platform, carries the messages between Zernio and your WhatsApp.</li>
      <li><strong>Helmcode</strong> turns your voice notes into text, on infrastructure in the European Union, without keeping them.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Zernio and Meta may process data outside the European Economic Area, under the
      European Commission’s standard contractual clauses or the EU–US Data Privacy Framework. Your use of WhatsApp itself
      is also covered by WhatsApp’s own terms and privacy policy.
    </p>

    <h2>On WhatsApp: how to stop it, and for how long</h2>
    <p>
      You can unlink your number in the app whenever you like, and we stop writing to you on WhatsApp. You can also ask your
      Rep to pause the search: until you resume it, it doesn’t write first, though it still answers you. The conversation is
      kept for as long as you have an account, so your Rep knows what you’ve talked about; it’s part of the “Your data”
      download and goes when you delete your account, with backups gone after 7 days, like everything else.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      You can ask to see, correct, export or delete your data, object to its use or restrict it, and withdraw your
      consent, by writing to <a href={mail}>{legal.privacyEmail}</a>. We answer within a month, usually much sooner. If
      you think we’ve handled your data badly, you can complain to the Spanish Data Protection Agency (AEPD) at{' '}
      <a href="https://www.aepd.es">aepd.es</a>, or to the authority where you live.
    </p>

    <h2>“Your turn” and GitHub</h2>
    <p>
      If you type a GitHub username in “Your turn”, your browser asks GitHub’s public API for that profile directly.
      The request never passes through us, and we don’t see or keep the username or the answer. GitHub’s own{' '}
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">privacy statement</a>{' '}
      applies to that request.
    </p>

    <h2>Counting visits</h2>
    <p>
      To know whether the page works, we count, per day: page views, how far down the page people get, and sign-ups.
      Only the totals are stored. No cookies, no identifiers, no IP addresses, and nothing that could tell us who you
      are. If your browser sends Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control, we don’t count you at all.
    </p>

    <h2>Cookies</h2>
    <p>
      None. The site sets no cookies and loads nothing from third parties on its own: fonts are served from our own
      domain. The only exception is the GitHub request above, and only when you ask for it. The app uses a single
      cookie, your session’s, which it needs to keep you signed in.
    </p>

    <p class="updated">Last updated {legal.updated}.</p>
  </Prose>
</Base>
